The duplicate values can cancel each other with XOR. A number XORed with itself becomes `0`, and XOR with `0` keeps the number unchanged. Since the order does not matter, one pass is enough.

For `[4, 1, 2, 1, 2]`, the running values are:

```text
0 ^ 4 = 4
4 ^ 1 = 5
5 ^ 2 = 7
7 ^ 1 = 6
6 ^ 2 = 4
```

The final value is the number that had no pair.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int singleNumber(vector<int>& nums){
        int result = 0;

        for (int number : nums) {
            result ^= number;
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the array is scanned once.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The input array is not changed.

A frequency map gives a direct way to solve this. Each number is counted while walking through the array. The answer is the key whose count is one.

This version is easy to follow and also works with negative values. Its trade-off is the map storage, which can grow with the number of different values.

```java
class Solution {
    public int singleNumber(int[] nums) {
        java.util.HashMap<Integer, Integer> frequency = new java.util.HashMap<>();

        for (int number : nums) {
            frequency.put(number, frequency.getOrDefault(number, 0) + 1);
        }

        for (int number : nums) {
            if (frequency.get(number) == 1) {
                return number;
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the frequency map. The input array is left unchanged.

The problem guarantees an answer, so the final `return 0` is only a fallback for the required method return type.

This keeps the logic very basic. For every value, another loop checks whether the same value appears at a different position. The first value without a matching position is returned.

It does not need a map or sorting, but checking the whole array again for each item makes it much slower on large inputs.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int singleNumber(vector<int>& nums){
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
            bool hasPair = false;

            for (int j = 0; j < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++j) {
                if (i != j && nums[i] == nums[j]) {
                    hasPair = true;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (!hasPair) {
                return nums[i];
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The input array is not modified.

The single-element case works too, because its only search never finds another index with the same value.

A set can hold the values that have appeared an odd number of times. When a number appears for the first time, it is added. When it appears again, it is removed. Since every repeated value appears twice, only the single value remains in the set.

Here is a short dry run for `[2, 3, 2]`:

```text
2: {2}
3: {2, 3}
2: {3}
```

The remaining item is returned with `pop()`.

```python
class Solution:
    def singleNumber(self, nums):
        remaining = set()

        for number in nums:
            if number in remaining:
                remaining.remove(number)
            else:
                remaining.add(number)

        return remaining.pop()
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average because set operations are usually constant time.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the set. The input array is not changed.

This also handles negative numbers and a one-item array. The method assumes the stated condition that exactly one value appears once and every other value appears twice.

